VALUES (
  '2026/05/22/optimism-bias-hormuz-the-cognitive-trap-driving-energy-investors',
  'behaviour', 'article',
  'The Optimism Bias Driving Energy Investors Into a Cognitive Trap',
  'Every rumour of a deal sends oil prices lower and energy stocks higher. Behavioural finance research explains why this pattern is dangerous, and why advisors who understand it are positioned to prevent a costly mistake.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Brent crude opened at $104.52 Friday,</strong><span> down from above $107 Thursday, as Secretary of State Rubio cited "encouraging signs" in Iran talks, sending energy-sensitive portfolios into another round of whipsaw repricing.</span></li>
<li><strong>The optimism bias, documented by Neil Weinstein in 1980 and extended into commodity markets by subsequent research,</strong><span> causes investors to systematically underweight adverse outcomes when official sources signal resolution, even when physical evidence points the other way.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Hormuz closure remains in effect.</strong><span> Approximately 10 million barrels per day of supply remains trapped, the ceasefire is described by President Trump as on "life support," and the EIA projects global inventories falling 8.5 million barrels per day through Q2 2026.</span></li>
<li><strong>For Canadian portfolios, the bias is compounding.</strong><span> TSX energy names have surged with war-elevated oil, and clients who bought into the thesis are now reluctant to question it, exhibiting classic confirmation bias alongside the optimism effect.</span></li>
<li><strong>The advisor intervention point is now.</strong><span> The gap between paper market pricing and physical market reality is the widest it has been since the crisis began, and clients who understand the cognitive mechanism are less likely to trade into the next rumour.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>When Secretary of State Marco Rubio said on Tuesday, May 20, that there were "encouraging signs" in U.S.-Iran negotiations, oil prices fell more than 2% within hours. By Thursday, May 21, they had recovered more than 3% as Iranian hardliners complicated the diplomatic picture. On Friday morning, Brent was trading at $104.52, down again. For an investor watching an energy-heavy portfolio reprice twice in three days on diplomat-speak, the temptation to conclude that resolution is imminent, and to act on that conclusion, is not irrational. It is exactly what the research predicts.</p>

<p>There is a cognitive phenomenon in behavioural finance called the optimism bias. Neil Weinstein first documented it formally in 1980, identifying the systematic human tendency to assign lower probabilities to negative outcomes than the actual data supports. Weinstein showed this was not random error but a structured cognitive pattern: people consistently overestimate the likelihood of positive resolution and underestimate the probability that adverse conditions persist. In commodity markets, this bias becomes self-reinforcing in a specific way. The more loudly official sources signal progress, the more aggressively speculative traders discount the physical evidence building in the opposite direction.</p>

<h2>The Physical Market Is Telling a Different Story</h2>

<p>The physical crude market in May 2026 has delivered an unambiguous verdict that the paper market keeps ignoring. Before the blockade took effect in late February, the Strait of Hormuz carried between 20 and 20.3 million barrels per day of crude oil and petroleum products, roughly 20% of global oil trade. Approximately 10 million barrels per day of that supply remains trapped as of this writing. The EIA''s May Short-Term Energy Outlook, released May 12, projects global inventories falling 8.5 million barrels per day through Q2 2026, keeping Brent prices around $106 per barrel through June. Strategic Petroleum Reserve drawdowns in the United States are at record levels: nearly 10 million barrels withdrawn in a single week in mid-May.</p>

<p>Yet each diplomatic rumour produces a price drop as if the strait were already open. The physical crude premium, which surged to more than $30 per barrel above Brent in early April 2026, has collapsed to near-parity as buyers step back. The market is not signalling that the crisis has eased. It is signalling that buyers, influenced by optimism bias, have temporarily stopped pricing the physical reality and started pricing the hoped-for diplomatic resolution.</p>

<h2>Confirmation Bias Is Compounding the Problem for Canadian Investors</h2>

<p>For clients who positioned into Canadian energy stocks during the early weeks of the crisis, a second cognitive bias has layered onto the optimism effect. The confirmation bias, extensively documented in the investment context by researchers including Barber and Odean in their 2008 work on trading behaviour, describes the tendency of investors to search for, interpret, and remember information that confirms their existing positions while discounting contradictory evidence.</p>

<p>A client who bought Suncor Energy at $87 or Canadian Natural Resources at $62 in March has a confirmation bias working against them now. Every Rubio statement about "encouraging signs" registers as evidence that the bet was right. Every hardliner statement from Tehran about keeping uranium stockpiles within Iran''s borders registers as temporary noise to be explained away. The cognitive asymmetry is structured: good news confirms the thesis, bad news gets a rationalization.</p>

<p>The chart above shows Brent crude''s price trajectory since the Hormuz closure, with diplomacy-driven drops overlaid against the sustained structural floor created by physical supply constraints. The pattern is consistent: each dip on diplomatic news has been followed by a recovery as the physical reality reasserts itself.</p>

