<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Each marked dip corresponds to a diplomatic statement from U.S. officials; all three dips were subsequently partially reversed as physical supply conditions reasserted themselves. The estimated $90 structural floor reflects analyst consensus on the level below which supply-constrained physical markets would quickly rebalance upward.</p>

<h2>What the Research Says About Managing This Pattern</h2>

<p>The Weinstein optimism bias research, and subsequent work by researchers including Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky on the planning fallacy, suggests a consistent finding: the bias is not corrected by providing more information. Telling a client that the Strait remains closed does not neutralize the optimism effect when the market is simultaneously pricing diplomatic progress. What does work, according to Kahneman''s 2011 synthesis in "Thinking, Fast and Slow," is pre-mortem analysis: asking the investor to assume the negative outcome has already occurred and to reconstruct the path by which it happened.</p>

<p>Applied to the current energy situation, the pre-mortem question is not "will Hormuz reopen?" It is "if the ceasefire collapses in June, what does your portfolio look like and what will you wish you had done in May?" This reframing forces engagement with the adverse scenario rather than allowing the optimism bias to suppress it. It is, notably, not a recommendation to reduce energy exposure. It is a process for stress-testing a position under conditions the client may be cognitively reluctant to examine.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with Canadian energy exposure are feeling two things simultaneously, and the combination is cognitively unstable. On one hand, the war-elevated oil prices have produced gains they can see in their statements, which generates satisfaction and anchors them to the current thesis. On the other hand, the daily price swings on diplomatic rumours are producing anxiety about whether to lock in gains or hold for further upside. The anxiety is real but its cause is misidentified: clients think they are responding to news, but they are responding to a confirmation bias loop that filters good news in and bad news out.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to walk you through something that I think is genuinely useful right now, not a sales pitch, just a framework. You''ve probably noticed that oil prices drop every time there''s a diplomatic headline about Iran, and then recover a few days later. That pattern has repeated three times in the past six weeks. What''s happening is that the paper market, the futures traders, keep pricing in a resolution that the physical market keeps contradicting. The physical supply constraint hasn''t changed. About ten million barrels a day of oil is still locked out of global markets. Every time a diplomat says something encouraging, traders get ahead of themselves, and the physical reality pulls prices back up. I want to ask you a question that may feel uncomfortable: if the ceasefire breaks down in June and oil spikes back toward $120, that''s obviously good for your energy holdings. But if negotiations stall into Q3 and the global economy starts pricing in a longer disruption, what do your fixed income and rate-sensitive positions look like? I want to make sure we''re stress-testing both sides of this, because right now the market is only stress-testing one of them.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who added energy names in March and April and are sitting on significant unrealized gains. They are the most vulnerable to confirmation bias because the position has already been validated by price appreciation. They need a pre-mortem conversation, not reassurance.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Balanced portfolio clients who held existing energy sector allocations before the crisis. The question for them is rebalancing: at what point does the energy overweight exceed the policy range, and has that conversation happened?</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who stayed diversified and are now questioning whether they missed the energy trade. The optimism bias literature is directly relevant here: they are more likely to chase the thesis now, at peak uncertainty, than they were in March when the physical case was clearest.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy sector weight for all clients against policy ranges, flag any portfolio where energy exceeds 10% of equity allocation without a documented rationale.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Run the pre-mortem question with any client who has added energy exposure since March 1: "If the ceasefire collapses in June, what does this portfolio look like?"</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who are asking about adding energy exposure now. Schedule a conversation before executing. The optimism bias risk is highest at this stage.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all energy-related conversations this week. If a client insists on adding exposure against your recommendation, document the conversation and the reasoning stated.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Energy markets and what the research tells us about this moment<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation today. I wanted to share one concept from behavioural finance that I think is particularly useful right now: the optimism bias. Research dating back to Neil Weinstein''s 1980 work shows that investors consistently underestimate the probability of adverse outcomes when official sources are signalling progress. The oil market is displaying this pattern in real time: prices drop on every diplomatic statement, then recover as the physical supply reality reasserts itself.<br><br>
This does not tell us which way Hormuz resolves. What it does tell us is that the market is not pricing the adverse scenario as carefully as it should be, and that our conversations should ensure that we are.<br><br>
I will be in touch as conditions develop. In the meantime, feel free to reach out with any questions.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY energy investors:</strong> Retail investors who bought Canadian energy names or energy ETFs (such as XEG.TO) in March or April are now experiencing cognitive whipsaw with each diplomatic headline. They have no one to help them think through the confirmation bias dynamic. This is exactly the gap an advisor fills.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with concentrated TSX exposure:</strong> Canadians who manage their own portfolios and are overweight TSX energy relative to any reasonable benchmark are carrying more Hormuz-scenario risk than they are likely aware of. The fact that the position has been profitable does not reduce the concentration risk.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees watching portfolio statements:</strong> Clients in or near drawdown who saw their energy-heavy statements improve in March are now watching daily swings. Without a framework for understanding the cognitive dynamics, they are likely to make reactive decisions at exactly the wrong moments.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m calling a handful of people this week because there''s something happening in energy markets that I think is worth a ten-minute conversation, not about what to do, but about what the research says is making this particular moment so cognitively difficult for investors without a framework.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The Hormuz-driven oil market is one of the most behaviorally complex investing environments in recent memory. Prices are moving on diplomatic rumours while the physical supply constraint is unchanged. That combination is designed to trigger the optimism bias and the confirmation bias simultaneously, and investors without a behavioural framework are navigating it with only price data and news headlines.</p>
<p>The advisor who understands what the research says about investor psychology in exactly this kind of environment can offer something that no financial app or online brokerage provides: the cognitive counterweight that keeps clients from trading into rumours and out of their stated long-term strategy.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you thinking about your Canadian energy exposure right now, given the swings we''ve seen this week?</p>
<p>When oil dropped on the diplomatic news Tuesday, what was your instinct? Did you want to reduce, add, or hold?</p>
<p>Have you stress-tested what your portfolio looks like if the ceasefire situation deteriorates rather than resolves over the next sixty days?</p>
<p>Who do you talk to when the market moves significantly in either direction?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Oil prices and the cognitive trap that''s catching investors this week<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
I''m reaching out because there''s a behavioural finance pattern playing out in energy markets right now that I think is worth understanding before it catches investors off guard.<br><br>
Every time a U.S. diplomat says something encouraging about Iran talks, oil prices drop 2%. Three days later, they recover. This has happened three times in six weeks. The market keeps pricing in resolution that the physical supply data keeps contradicting.<br><br>
Researchers call this the optimism bias. When official sources signal progress, investors systematically underweight the probability that the adverse scenario persists. It is one of the most well-documented patterns in behavioural finance, and it tends to produce the worst outcomes when investors act on it rather than understand it.<br><br>
I''d be happy to walk through what the research says about managing this dynamic and what it might mean for Canadian energy positions. No obligation, just a conversation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

