VALUES (
  '2026/05/22/energy-stock-gains-tfsa-rrsp-the-tax-move-most-advisors-are-missing',
  'tax', 'article',
  'Energy Stock Gains, TFSA Room, and the Tax Move Most Advisors Are Missing',
  'Canadian energy names have surged 40% or more since March. Clients sitting on those gains outside registered accounts face a decision with real tax consequences. The planning window is open now, and most advisors haven''t started the conversation.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>TSX energy stocks have surged sharply since the Hormuz closure began March 4,</strong><span> with names like Suncor (SU.TO) trading near $93 and Canadian Natural Resources (CNQ.TO) near $67, representing substantial unrealized gains for clients who held or added exposure in the early weeks of the crisis.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canada''s capital gains inclusion rate remains at 50% for individuals,</strong><span> after the Carney government formally cancelled the proposed increase to 66.67% in March 2025. That cancellation is now legislation, and it changes the planning calculus for clients weighing whether to realize gains this year.</span></li>
<li><strong>The 2026 TFSA annual limit is $7,000, unchanged from 2025,</strong><span> and cumulative room for anyone eligible since 2009 who has never contributed now stands at $102,000. Clients who have room available and are holding appreciated energy names in taxable accounts have a clear planning opportunity.</span></li>
<li><strong>The RRSP 2026 contribution limit is $33,810,</strong><span> based on 18% of 2025 earned income up to that cap. Unused room carries forward. For clients who have not maximized contributions, the interaction between energy-driven income and RRSP room has direct planning implications.</span></li>
<li><strong>The planning conversation is account-type specific.</strong><span> Whether a client should realize gains now, shelter future growth in a TFSA, or use RRSP room to offset income depends entirely on their account structure, marginal rate, and whether the energy thesis has further to run.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>When oil prices move 60% in ten weeks, the planning conversation changes. Canadian energy stocks have been among the strongest performers on the TSX since the Hormuz closure began on March 4, with integrated producers like Suncor Energy and Canadian Natural Resources delivering substantial unrealized gains to clients who held or added energy exposure in the early weeks of the conflict. For clients sitting on those gains outside registered accounts, a tax planning window is open that most advisors have not yet addressed explicitly.</p>

<p>The starting point is the capital gains inclusion rate. After months of uncertainty, the Carney government cancelled the proposed increase to 66.67% in March 2025, and the rate for individuals remains at 50%. That means a client who realizes a $100,000 capital gain on an energy stock today includes $50,000 in taxable income. For a client in Ontario at the top marginal rate of approximately 53.53%, the federal-provincial tax on that gain is roughly $26,765. That calculation does not change at the end of this calendar year under current law.</p>

<h2>The TFSA as the First Conversation</h2>

<p>The most immediate planning tool available to most clients is the Tax-Free Savings Account. The 2026 annual limit is $7,000, unchanged from 2024 and 2025. Cumulative room for a Canadian who has been eligible since the TFSA''s inception in 2009 and has never contributed now stands at $102,000. The planning move that most advisors are not making is this: for clients who hold appreciated energy names in a taxable account and have significant TFSA room available, the question is not just "should we realize these gains" but "where does the next dollar of energy exposure live?"</p>

<p>A client who sells appreciated energy units in a taxable account, realizes the gain at the 50% inclusion rate, and then contributes the proceeds to a TFSA to repurchase the position has converted future growth on those assets to permanently tax-free status. The cost is the current tax on the realized gain. The benefit is that all future appreciation, dividends, and income on those assets inside the TFSA are never taxed again, including on withdrawal. For clients who believe the energy thesis has further to run through the Hormuz disruption, this is not a call to exit energy. It is a call to ensure that the next leg of appreciation accrues inside the right account.</p>

<p>The chart above shows the TFSA cumulative room available by year of first eligibility, illustrating the magnitude of the opportunity for clients who have not maximized contributions.</p>

<div class="hdq-chart">
