<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">A client eligible since 2009 who has never contributed has $102,000 of TFSA room available as of January 1, 2026. Even partial use of that room to shelter energy-driven appreciation materially changes the long-run tax profile of the portfolio.</p>

<h2>RRSP Room and the Income Interaction</h2>

<p>The second account-type question involves RRSP room. The 2026 RRSP contribution limit is $33,810, based on 18% of 2025 earned income up to that ceiling. For clients who have not maximized RRSP contributions, the interaction with energy-sector income this year has two dimensions.</p>

<p>First, clients who hold energy names that pay elevated dividends inside a taxable account are generating additional taxable income in 2026. That income creates RRSP room for the following year, but it also means their 2026 marginal rate calculation for any capital gain realization will be higher than in a normal year. Sequencing matters: advisors who discuss RRSP contributions before advising on whether to realize energy gains in a taxable account are giving their clients a more complete picture.</p>

<p>Second, clients who hold energy positions inside an RRSP benefit from the full pre-tax compounding of the position but will pay full marginal rates on withdrawal. For clients in or near retirement, the question of whether to hold high-growth energy names inside the RRSP or inside the TFSA depends on whether they expect to be in a lower or higher marginal rate bracket at withdrawal. The Hormuz-driven appreciation makes this a live question for clients who were carrying energy positions in the wrong account type for their retirement income plan.</p>

<h2>The Corporate Account Dimension</h2>

<p>For incorporated clients, the capital gains inclusion rate applies to all gains realized by the corporation at 66.67%, not the 50% rate available to individuals on the first $250,000 of annual gains. That asymmetry, which has been in place since January 1, 2026, means incorporated business owners holding appreciated energy positions in a Canadian-controlled private corporation pay a materially higher effective rate on realization than individual clients. The Capital Dividend Account mechanism provides a partial offset, but the planning conversation for incorporated clients is distinct from the individual client analysis and should not be conflated with it.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with energy gains are feeling a combination of satisfaction and indecision. The gains are real and visible, which is gratifying. But the diplomatic volatility in oil prices is creating uncertainty about timing: clients are asking whether they should "lock in" before something goes wrong, or "hold on" for further upside. The tax dimension is often absent from their thinking entirely. They are making a timing decision without the account-type framework that would make that decision rational.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to walk through something that I think is going to make a real difference to how you think about these energy gains. Before we talk about whether to sell or hold, I want to ask about your account structure. Where are these positions sitting right now? Because the tax answer to "should I realize this gain" is completely different depending on whether we''re talking about a taxable account, an RRSP, or a TFSA. If you''re holding appreciated Suncor or CNQ units in a taxable account and you have unused TFSA room, there''s a specific move we should be discussing that has nothing to do with your view on oil prices. It''s about making sure the next leg of this trade, wherever it goes, accrues in the most tax-efficient account available to you. Can you tell me what TFSA room you think you have right now?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Individual clients holding appreciated energy names in non-registered accounts with significant unused TFSA room. The gain-and-recontribute planning opportunity is directly available and time-sensitive.</p>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Incorporated clients with energy positions in a CCPC. The 66.67% inclusion rate at the corporate level makes this a different and more urgent conversation than for individual clients.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients approaching retirement who hold energy names inside an RRSP. The account-type question for them is whether these high-growth assets should be migrated to a TFSA to avoid full marginal rate taxation on RRIF withdrawals.</p>
<p><strong>Lower impact:</strong> Clients whose energy exposure is entirely inside registered accounts with no further tax optimization available without triggering other planning consequences.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull a TFSA room summary for all clients with meaningful energy exposure in taxable accounts. Compare available room against the size of the unrealized gain. Any client with more than $20,000 in unused room and more than $20,000 in unrealized taxable gains is a priority conversation this week.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag all incorporated clients with energy positions held inside a CCPC for a separate review. The 66.67% corporate inclusion rate changes the urgency and the math.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For retirement-horizon clients, map which energy positions are in RRSP versus TFSA versus taxable. The Hormuz-driven appreciation may have created a rebalancing opportunity across account types that the original plan did not anticipate.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do not combine the TFSA and RRSP conversations in a single call. They involve different trade-offs and clients need to process them separately. Book a follow-up if necessary.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your energy gains and the account-type question we should address<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation. I want to make sure we''ve looked at your energy positions through both lenses: the market timing question you''re thinking about, and the account-type question that I think is equally important right now.<br><br>
The short version: the tax outcome for realized energy gains depends entirely on where those positions are sitting. For gains in a non-registered account, we should review your available TFSA room before making any decision. For incorporated clients, the corporate inclusion rate is materially higher than the individual rate, which changes the urgency of the planning conversation.<br><br>
I''ll be in touch with specifics once I''ve reviewed your current account structure. If you have questions in the meantime, please reach out.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with energy gains:</strong> Self-directed investors who bought energy ETFs or individual names in March are now sitting on substantial gains with no tax plan. They are making timing decisions without account-type analysis. This is a natural prospecting conversation.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with investment holdings in a CCPC:</strong> Incorporated professionals who hold energy names inside their corporation face the 66.67% inclusion rate and may not be aware that the individual rate is materially different. This is a high-value planning conversation.</p>
<p><strong>Pre-retirees with RRSP-heavy portfolios:</strong> Clients approaching retirement whose energy exposure sits entirely in an RRSP may be accumulating future RRIF tax liability on high-growth assets when TFSA would serve them better. This cohort often does not have an advisor specifically focused on account-type optimization.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m reaching out because there''s a tax planning move that''s become relevant for a lot of investors who added Canadian energy exposure earlier this year, and it''s one that most people aren''t thinking about. It has nothing to do with whether you believe oil goes higher from here. It''s about which account those gains live in.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The Hormuz-driven energy rally has created an unusual situation: a large number of Canadian investors are sitting on significant capital gains in positions they intend to hold for further upside. The tax optimization question, specifically which account those assets should be in, is completely independent of the market view. A DIY investor focused on oil price direction is almost certainly not asking the account-type question. That is the gap an advisor fills.</p>
<p>For incorporated clients, the gap is even wider. The difference between the individual rate at 50% inclusion and the corporate rate at 66.67% is a planning conversation that has a specific dollar value attached to it. Identifying that value for a prospect is a concrete demonstration of advisory worth that no robo-advisor or online brokerage provides.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Where are your Canadian energy positions sitting right now, inside a registered account or outside?</p>
<p>Have you thought about how you''d handle the tax side if you decided to take some of those gains off the table?</p>
<p>Do you know how much TFSA room you have available right now?</p>
<p>If you''re incorporated, are you holding any of these positions inside your corporation?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Canadian energy gains and a tax question most investors aren''t asking<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
If you''ve been holding Canadian energy names since the Hormuz closure began in March, you''re likely sitting on significant gains. Most investors in that position are focused entirely on the market timing question: hold for more upside, or take some off the table?<br><br>
There''s a second question that''s at least as important, and most investors aren''t asking it: which account are those gains sitting in, and is it the right one?<br><br>
The answer changes the tax math materially, and it''s different depending on whether you''re an individual investor, near retirement, or incorporated. I''d be happy to walk through the account-type analysis for your specific situation. No obligation, just a conversation that I think will be useful regardless of what you decide to do with the positions.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

