</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors monitoring energy positions:</strong> Self-directed investors who have been following the Hormuz situation through daily headlines are receiving signal and noise in equal measure with no framework to separate them. The four-scenario structure is a concrete demonstration of advisor value.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with supply chain exposure:</strong> Canadian businesses that import goods affected by global shipping costs have a direct operational stake in the Hormuz outcome. That operational concern is a natural entry point for a broader financial planning conversation.</p>
<p><strong>Investors approaching major liquidity events:</strong> Clients planning a business sale, property sale, or large portfolio withdrawal in 2026 need to understand how each Hormuz scenario affects the timing and tax implications of that event. The scenario framework makes this concrete.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m calling because most of the Hormuz coverage treats this as a binary situation, deal or no deal, and I think that framing is making it harder for investors to think clearly about their portfolios. There are actually four scenarios worth planning for, and where current oil prices sit tells us something specific about which ones the market is and is not pricing.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The Hormuz situation is producing one of the most complex investing environments Canadian advisors have navigated in recent years. The complexity is not just geopolitical. It is informational: a constant flow of diplomatic headlines that move markets without resolving the underlying structure of the negotiation. An advisor who can organize that complexity into a four-scenario framework, and connect each scenario to specific portfolio implications, is doing something that no financial app or news aggregator does. That is the value on offer in this conversation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
