VALUES (
  '2026/05/22/tsx-holds-34410-as-diplomacy-dip-pattern-reasserts-itself',
  'market', 'article',
  'TSX Holds Near 34,410 as the Diplomacy-Dip Pattern Reasserts Itself',
  'The TSX gained 248 points Thursday on ceasefire optimism and easing Treasury yields. Friday morning opens flat as Brent pulls back to $104.52 on the same optimism. The pattern has now repeated three times in six weeks, and the market is pricing resolution that the physical supply data keeps contradicting.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The TSX Composite closed at 34,410 Thursday, up 248 points or 0.7%,</strong><span> driven by consumer cyclicals, financials, and utilities as easing U.S. Treasury yields reduced pressure on rate-sensitive sectors. The index is up 1.7% on the week and near its highest level since the March conflict shock.</span></li>
<li><strong>Friday morning opens flat,</strong><span> with a muted crude oil and metals complex capping upside. Brent crude is at $104.52, down 1.89% from Thursday after Secretary of State Rubio''s "encouraging signs" comment on Iran talks pulled oil lower before the session opened in Toronto.</span></li>
<li><strong>The diplomacy-dip pattern has now repeated three times since the April 8 ceasefire:</strong><span> April 8 ceasefire announcement, May 6 Rubio statement, and May 20 Rubio statement each produced a 2% to 6% crude selloff that was subsequently partially reversed as physical supply conditions reasserted the structural floor.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canadian energy names are the key variable for the TSX today.</strong><span> Suncor (SU.TO) trades near $93.30, Canadian Natural Resources (CNQ.TO) near $67.11, and Cenovus (CVE.TO) near $42. Each is sensitive to any further Brent softness on Friday as the week closes.</span></li>
<li><strong>Gold at $4,524 USD per ounce is down 0.42% Friday</strong><span> as the same diplomatic optimism that is softening oil is reducing safe-haven demand. Agnico Eagle (AEM.TO) and Barrick Gold are watching for direction into the close.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The TSX Composite closed at 34,410 Thursday, its best level since before the March conflict shock, lifted by a broad-based rally that had more to do with U.S. Treasury yields easing than with any fundamental change in the Hormuz supply picture. Consumer cyclicals, financials, and utilities led the advance as the rate-sensitive sectors that had been under pressure through the hold environment found relief in softer bond yields. Friday morning, the index opens roughly flat as crude oil pulls back and the diplomacy-dip pattern reasserts itself for the third time in six weeks.</p>

<p>Brent crude is at $104.52 Friday morning, down $1.99 or 1.89% from Thursday''s close above $106. The catalyst is the same catalyst that produced the April 8 and May 6 dips: Secretary of State Marco Rubio said on Wednesday that there were "encouraging signs" in U.S.-Iran negotiations, that Pakistani mediators are travelling to Tehran, and that Washington''s latest proposal had "partially bridged" the gap between the two sides. Oil traders sold the news. By the time Toronto opens, the question for Friday''s TSX session is whether energy names, which led the Thursday rally, give back ground on the crude pullback or hold their levels on the view that Rubio''s language is another head fake.</p>

<h2>The Energy Sector Is Carrying the Week''s Gains</h2>

<p>Canadian energy names have been the primary driver of the TSX''s 1.7% weekly gain. Suncor Energy trades near $93.30, up sharply from its pre-war level of approximately $57 in early January, an advance of more than 63% that reflects both the oil price surge and the market''s repricing of integrated Canadian producer economics at sustained $100-plus crude. Canadian Natural Resources trades near $67.11. Cenovus Energy trades near $42, with RBC Capital raising its price target to C$47 this week based on refining margin strength and elevated crude pricing.</p>

<p>The vulnerability in Friday''s session is straightforward. If Brent continues lower through the morning on further diplomatic optimism, energy names will reprice downward and the TSX will give back a portion of Thursday''s gain. The structural question is how much of Thursday''s broad-based advance was genuine risk appetite improvement versus mechanical repricing driven by Treasury yield moves that are themselves Hormuz-sensitive. Lower yields on diplomatic progress, higher yields on conflict escalation: the rate-sensitive sectors are also being driven by the same geopolitical variable as the energy sector, just through a different transmission mechanism.</p>

<p>The chart above shows the TSX Composite weekly close since the Hormuz closure began March 4, with the three diplomacy-driven crude dips marked and the energy sector''s contribution to the index highlighted.</p>

