<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX has recovered 2,230 points or 6.9% from its March 9 conflict low of 31,420, closing Thursday at 34,410. Each of the three marked diplomatic events produced a short-term crude selloff that temporarily suppressed TSX energy names before the physical supply floor reasserted itself.</p>

<h2>The CAD and Gold Complex</h2>

<p>The Canadian dollar is trading near 0.728 USD Friday, little changed on the week and sitting in the narrow range it has occupied since mid-May. The CAD has been the most compressed expression of the Hormuz uncertainty: it benefits from elevated oil through Canada''s terms of trade but is simultaneously weighed by the risk-off environment and the Bank of Canada''s hold, which provides no rate differential support against the U.S. dollar. Each diplomatic rumour of resolution softens oil, weakens the CAD''s commodity support, and produces a modest dip that reverses when the physical supply reality reasserts.</p>

<p>Gold at $4,524 USD per ounce is down 0.42% Friday, on track for a roughly flat week after falling 3.7% over the past month from its late-April highs. The same diplomatic optimism reducing oil demand is reducing safe-haven demand for gold. Tehran''s statement that the latest U.S. proposal had "partially bridged" the gap between the two sides sent gold lower in overnight trading. Agnico Eagle Mines, Canada''s largest senior gold producer, and Wheaton Precious Metals are both watching for directional cues into the Friday close. Gold remains 34.7% above its level a year ago, anchored by the structural geopolitical premium the Hormuz crisis has embedded.</p>

<h2>What the Week''s Pattern Tells the Market</h2>

<p>The most useful read of this week is not the 248-point Thursday gain or Friday''s flat open. It is the underlying sector rotation that the week produced. Consumer cyclicals and financials led Thursday''s advance on yield relief, not on any improvement in the Hormuz supply picture. Energy names participated but were not the primary driver. That rotation, from energy-led gains driven by oil prices to rate-sensitive gains driven by bond yields, suggests the TSX is beginning to price two separate variables simultaneously: the geopolitical premium in energy, and the domestic monetary policy path that operates independently of it.</p>

<p>For Canadian advisors, that two-variable structure is the most important market development of the week. A TSX that was purely an oil proxy would be simple to position around. A TSX that is simultaneously responding to oil prices and to rate expectations, and where those two variables are themselves correlated through the Hormuz-inflation-BoC transmission mechanism, requires a more nuanced portfolio construction conversation than most clients are currently having.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients watching the TSX this week are feeling cautiously positive after Thursday''s 248-point gain but are unsettled by Friday''s flat open following what looked like good diplomatic news. The disconnect between "encouraging signs" on Iran and a market that does not sustainably rally on those signs is producing confusion. Clients with energy exposure are watching daily price swings that are larger than anything they experienced in a normal year and are unsure whether to interpret Thursday''s broad rally as a signal that the worst is over or as another head fake.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Thursday was a good day for the TSX, and I want to give you an honest read on what drove it. The 248-point gain was real, but it was led by financials and utilities, not primarily by energy. What happened is that U.S. Treasury yields eased on the diplomatic headlines about Iran, and that yield move lifted the rate-sensitive sectors that had been under pressure. Energy names participated, but they were not the story. The reason Friday is opening flat is that the same diplomatic optimism that pushed yields down and rate-sensitive stocks up also pushed oil down, and now the energy names are under a bit of pressure. This is the third time we have seen this pattern since the ceasefire on April 8. Each time, oil pulls back on a diplomatic statement, the market prices a resolution that has not happened yet, and then the physical supply reality pulls oil back up within a few days. The TSX at 34,410 is 6.9% above its March 9 low. That recovery has been real and it has been earned. What I would caution against is reading Thursday''s broad-based rally as a signal that the Hormuz situation is resolved, because the negotiating gap on the core issues has not changed.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients concentrated in TSX energy names who are watching daily oil price moves as a proxy for portfolio health. They need the two-variable framework: energy is one driver, rate sensitivity is another, and this week showed both operating simultaneously.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Balanced portfolio clients whose fixed income positions have been affected by the yield volatility that runs through the same Hormuz-inflation-BoC transmission. The bond market is not a refuge from Hormuz; it is a different expression of the same variable.</p>
<p><strong>Potential opportunity:</strong> Clients who have been sitting in cash or cash equivalents waiting for "clarity" on Hormuz. The TSX at 34,410 is 6.9% above its March low. The window for buying the conflict dip has partially closed, but the scenario framework suggests it has not fully closed in the prolonged closure or ceasefire collapse scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review all client portfolios for the two-variable exposure: energy sector weight and rate-sensitive sector weight. Clients who are overweight both are carrying correlated Hormuz risk through two different transmission mechanisms.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients who asked about "buying the dip" in March and did not act, have the scenario conversation today. The TSX has recovered significantly but has not priced the ceasefire collapse scenario. That is where the asymmetric risk still sits.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check Lightspeed Commerce (LSPD.TO) positions. The stock fell 6.6% Thursday on a disappointing fiscal 2027 outlook. Any client holding it should have received a call before today.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note gold producer positions into the Friday close. If gold gives back further ground on diplomatic optimism, Agnico Eagle and Wheaton will move with it. Assess whether current gold allocations are sized as a geopolitical hedge or as a return driver, because the two sizing frameworks are different.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
