  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors who bought the conflict dip:</strong> Self-directed investors who added TSX energy exposure in March are now sitting on significant gains and are watching daily oil price moves with no framework for what to do next. Thursday''s broad rally and Friday''s flat open in the same week is exactly the kind of confusing signal that creates an opening for an advisor conversation.</p>
<p><strong>Investors sitting in cash:</strong> Canadians who moved to cash or GICs during the March shock and are now watching the TSX at 34,410 are experiencing regret and are asking whether they have missed the recovery. The honest answer, which the scenario framework supports, is that they have partially missed it but not entirely, depending on which scenario plays out.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees in drawdown watching portfolio volatility:</strong> Clients taking regular withdrawals who have been watching the TSX swing 500 to 800 points in both directions over the past ten weeks need a framework for understanding the volatility, not just reassurance that "markets recover."</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m calling because Thursday was the best TSX day in weeks and Friday is opening flat on what looks like good news from Iran, and I think that disconnect is worth a ten-minute conversation. What''s happening in the market right now is more nuanced than the headlines suggest, and I think it has specific implications for how you should be thinking about your Canadian equity exposure.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The TSX is currently being driven by two variables simultaneously: oil prices through the energy sector, and rate expectations through the financials, utilities, and rate-sensitive sectors. Both variables are downstream of the same Hormuz situation, but they move in different directions on the same diplomatic news. An advisor who can explain that two-variable structure, and connect it to a client''s specific portfolio, is doing something that no news article or financial app delivers. The investor who understands why Thursday was a strong day and Friday is flat, despite good diplomatic news, is the investor who does not make a reactive decision at the wrong moment.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How did you feel watching Thursday''s 248-point gain followed by a flat open Friday on diplomatic progress, did that make sense to you or did it feel contradictory?</p>
<p>How much of your Canadian equity exposure is in energy names versus rate-sensitive sectors like financials and utilities?</p>
<p>If the TSX pulls back 5% from here on a diplomatic setback, does your current plan tell you what to do, or would you be making a judgment call in the moment?</p>
<p>Who do you talk to when the market does something that does not make obvious sense?</p>
</div>
